Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary drama; naturalistic faces, fabric, and Milwaukee/Seattle locations; 1990s into early 2000s period detail.

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A fifth-floor brick walk-up in downtown Milwaukee. Wind pushes trash along the alley. Garbage-can lids mark kickball bases.

(SOUND: distant traffic, wind in the alley)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not the book. My real Big Brother. Nick.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Cramped living room. MA, older, brown hair going gray, sits near the TV in a worn chair. PETER, late twenties, lean, ordinary clothes, carries grocery bags. NICK fills the doorway — tall, rough-scarred face, reddish-brown hair uncombed, chin and left-eyebrow scars, steel presence.

(SOUND: TV game-show chatter, bags thudding on counter)

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill.

Nick sets cash on the counter and leaves. Peter watches the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. A modern-day Viking. I lived with him and Ma after Dad took off.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Nick shoulders through the door. A lifter mid-curl drops a forty-pound bar and steps aside. Nick nods, takes the bench, loads fifty.

(SOUND: iron plates clanging, gym echo)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When he walked in, people moved. I know he'll stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

Peter locks a bike. SIONNA — short brown hair, pale skin, light-blue eyes, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, backpack — trips on cement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: street noise, soft stumble of shoes)

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. Their eyes hold.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon. I don't eat pizza. If you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes a number. Walks away, glances back over her shoulder.

(SOUND: her footsteps fading)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Intense. Like waves at the ocean covering me, and I didn't want to move.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma lines pill bottles, glassy-eyed, mumbling.

(SOUND: bottles clicking, fridge hum)

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta?

PETER
Ma, we don't go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back, softens.

MA
Oh, right. I am so sorry.

Peter grabs a jacket.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers on TV. Nick slaps Peter's back. Pool balls crack in the next room.

(SOUND: bar crowd, TV football, cue ball strike)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

PETER
Ma was all confused. Church. Mrs. Canasta.

NICK
I knew she'd go crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. I don't have money for a nursing home.

Nick's look hardens. Peter pays and leaves.

EXT. CITY LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter hurries up wet steps under old lights.

(SOUND: rain on stone, door hinge)

INT. CITY LIBRARY - NIGHT

Wood floors, chain-hung lamps, classic stacks. Peter fills a card at the desk, pulls Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: pages riffling, quiet footsteps)

LIBRARIAN
Plastic card in a week.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when everything was free in this building?

INT. PETER'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Peter reads under a bare bulb. Falls asleep. Dream light: forest, brown mare, linen sleeves, Sionna in old dress, light-blue eyes. First kiss in a grassy field.

(SOUND: soft horse breath, then sudden gasp)

Peter bolts upright, sweating.

PETER
Hold!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sionna's face. Old clothes. What is happening?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College crowd, backpacks. Sionna at a back table, green hooded top, watery blue eyes. Peter sits.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low chatter)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it. Ghosts. Double characters. The moors. A love that never changes destroys you.

Peter leans in, awake.

PETER
Something inside me is coming awake.

SIONNA
Dinner Friday?

He nods. Outside at the bus stop he walks lighter.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Maybe that was Ma's problem. Dad left and she never moved on. I would never forgive him. Nick became my dad when I was eight.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Peter in t-shirt and shorts tucks the shirt in, struggles through poses, breathless. Sionna beside him, calm. Final savasana — stillness.

(SOUND: instructor's soft count, collective breathing)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
I felt so dumb. But after... calm. Nothing bothered me at work.

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. That's why they call it practice.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Nick stomps, face red, fists flexing. Ma sits blank in her chair.

(SOUND: door slam echo, floorboards)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I met a girl. We had a couple dates.

NICK
A girl. Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out. Peter stares at the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? In my mind I was trying to figure how Ma, Nick, Sionna could fit.

INT. DOCTOR'S OFFICE - DAY

DR. CARSON faces Peter. Grey sky in the window.

(SOUND: distant siren, clock tick)

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Take her to the emergency room.

PETER
I'll call Nick.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma dozes on monitors. Peter sleeps in a chair. Dream: battlefield chaos, blood, a dying man whispers "Save her." Nick runs in and drives a long knife into Peter's chest.

(SOUND: monitor beep, Peter's sharp inhale)

Peter wakes gasping. Sionna later walks him outside; his face eases.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think I loved her. I just hadn't told her yet.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Blue weekly pill box on the table. Nick smirks, boots on the footstool.

(SOUND: TV low, boots on wood)

NICK
Aren't you nurse Nancy. This chick sure has you wrapped.

PETER
You haven't even met her. Give her a break.

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

PETER
You're so goddamn hateful.

Nick slaps Peter's head. Ear rings. Door slams.

(SOUND: open-hand slap, door crash)

PETER
(whisper)
Me and Ma and Sionna. Fine without him.

EXT. SCHOOLYARD - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Younger Nick, sixth grade, steps between laughing kids and MRS. STARK. Protects little Peter.

(SOUND: kids yelling, recess bell)

YOUNG NICK
I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It made me love him. It made me hate him.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Months later. Peter tells Nick about community college and a student loan. Sionna's name hangs in the air. Ma watches TV, uneasy.

(SOUND: cop-show theme, tense silence)

NICK
This chick tell you what to do? Bro's before ho's. Nothin' comes between us.

PETER
Sure, Nick. I get you.

INT. JOE'S BAR POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Crowded Saturday. AC/DC on the speakers. Nick eyes LINDSEY with a dark-haired date. Rage builds. Peter steps in front of him.

(SOUND: rock music, pool-room hush, fists flexing)

PETER
Leave it, man. You don't even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it. You're next.

Nick's fist draws back in slow motion. Joe and a bouncer rush in. Chaos. Peter runs into the cold night, lungs burning, to Sionna's door.

(SOUND: head-butt thud, running footsteps, heartbeat)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Peter collapses on the green suede sofa, head in her lap. She strokes his hair.

(SOUND: distant traffic, soft breathing)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares does that. You are becoming a bravely compassionate being.

PETER
The first time I met you I felt like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too. The purpose of your life is to find the true self within you.

They sleep. Dream: Sionna rises from a lake as an angel; giant Nick with a mace shrinks and vanishes under her force.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Sionna serves pie. Nick finally meets her — thin smile, refuses sugar. She answers his challenge on women in college with calm facts. He leaves poisoned.

(SOUND: forks on plates, door close)

NICK
(whisper at door)
Take care of Ma.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the best of times and the worst of times. Nick was in. I was out.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Sionna drives the Civic through green Wisconsin hills toward her parents' farm. Peter rides shotgun, lighter.

(SOUND: tires on asphalt, wind at windows)

SIONNA
Just call them Mushroom and Ginger.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Last normal night. Homework, chips, sleep. Morning after: empty.

(SOUND: soft rain starting)

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Stillness. No Ma. Answering machine blinks.

(SOUND: machine beep)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, Milwaukee County Sheriff. We need to talk regarding your family.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Stone building, 1898 plaque. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, faces shaking Peter.

(SOUND: station murmur, styrofoam cup set down)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition. Your mother passed away at the scene. Toxicology pending. Possible alcohol.

Peter's hands shake on cold water.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Nick unconscious, chest tube, broken femur, bruised. Peter looks through glass, turns away.

(SOUND: monitor alarms, staff shoes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Peter breaks down at Ma's empty chair, screaming into the space. Sionna holds him.

(SOUND: raw sobbing, rain on glass)

SIONNA
Just let it out. We'll figure it out.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / CEMETERY - DAY

Modest casket. Few mourners. Dirt hits the lid. Marker: Olivia Anne Olsen. 2 Timothy 4:7.

(SOUND: muted thunk of dirt, wind in trees)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Days later. Nick yellow-bruised, weak. Tears.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, TV mute click)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up. Three Jack and Cokes. Vehicular manslaughter. Mistake.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Peter walks out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I no longer gave two shits about Nick.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Grey autumn. Nick forces in — scarred, raging, switchblade. Pins Sionna to the wall, blade at collarbone.

(SOUND: door knock, cat scurry, blade snick open)

NICK
You're the reason I'm going to jail. Break it off. Cash in a week. Or this blade.

Front door bangs. Peter lunges. Struggle. Blade bites. Blood. Nick elbows Peter, wipes the knife, flees.

(SOUND: body thud, wet gasp, running feet)

PETER
Holy shit, Nick!

Peter clamps the wound, dials 911, blood on his hands.

(SOUND: sirens approaching, his ragged breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If I hadn't walked in, she would have died. Her savior who couldn't save himself.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY (MONTHS LATER)

Sionna in the rocking chair, blank ocean eyes, shoulder stiff from repair. Peter kneels.

(SOUND: clock tick, winter wind)

SIONNA
I can't feel me anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe a break. Mom and Dad's.

PETER
Stay. We can work it out.

She walks to the bedroom. He leaves into the void.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY (YEARS LATER)

Grey blanket sky, mist, hoods. PETER, older, steadier, walks Belltown past a keyboard player. Signage: Tony's Boy's Pizza.

(SOUND: light rain, ferry horn distant, keyboard riff)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years. My own chain. Hatred for my brother. I miss her smile.

INT. LOWELL'S / PIKE PLACE - DAY

Peter at a window table, cinnamon roll like a halo. Mountains hidden in cloud. Memory hits: blood hallway, 911, Sionna pale.

(SOUND: market crowd below, cup set down)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She blamed me in the end. I finished the degree. Built the business. Still the void.

INT. YOGA STUDIO SEATTLE - NIGHT

Candles, citrus-lavender. Gratefulness theme. Peter on a mat among ten others.

(SOUND: soft chant, mats unrolling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Shakespeare — thinking makes it so. I still feel sorrow for him. DNA demands it.

INT. PETER'S SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Cat Bozo on the couch. Peter dreams: sage on a green road, dirty pizza box. Sage: stop judging; all things illusion; create your reality. Peter wakes smiling, writes it down.

(SOUND: cat purr, pen on paper)

PETER
I have to find forgiveness.

INT. COFFEE SHOP - DAY

Blonde barista CHRIS, hoop earrings, apron. Peter asks her out to a play. Sun breaks the drizzle.

(SOUND: milk steamer, soft laugh)

INT. AIRPLANE / MILWAUKEE STREETS - DAY

Peter lands, rents a Camry. Old brick fortress on Kirk. Alley dumpsters. Former pizza parlor now cosmetology school. Deli: Bob, grayer, thicker, bear-hugs him.

(SOUND: jet cabin, then street, Bob's loud cackle)

BOB
You got out and did something. Good for you. Nick looks sad. I couldn't visit no more.

PETER
I need to let the shit go.

EXT. SIONNA'S OLD DUPLEX - DAY

Greyer paint, naked porch. Peter sits in the car, remembers bamboo rockers, Smiley the cat, air kiss.

(SOUND: kids on skateboards, his quiet breath)

PETER
(to mirror)
You can't look back. You are moving forward.

EXT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL - DAY

Oppressive brick. Metal detectors. Peter waits, coffee shaking slightly.

(SOUND: locks clanging, distant voices)

INT. PRISON VISIT BOOTH - DAY

Plexiglass. Phone receivers. NICK enters — older, graying, scrawny, bagged sad eyes. They stare.

(SOUND: amplified room tone, phone pickup)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came. Parole maybe two years.

PETER
I'm here to tell you some things. Just listen. You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

Nick's eyes fill.

PETER
And this is the most important bit. I forgive you. It sets me free. Releases your control forever. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother. I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't. I did it.

NICK
I'm so sorry. You're a big man. I'm a small one. President Olson. I am so proud of you. I love you.

Guard signals time. Nick is led away. Peter hangs up, light.

(SOUND: door buzz, footsteps receding)

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT - DAY

Rolling green hills. Peter drives free. At the gate, boarding pass scanned.

(SOUND: tires humming, airport murmur)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The weight of thirty years gone. Somewhere she's happy. I send him love and hope. I will never see him again by choice.

ATTENDANT
Boarding pass?

Peter looks at the name.

PETER
(whisper)
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He walks down the jet bridge into light.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
